menu "I2S loopback test"

choice DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_ROLE
    prompt "Role for this board"
    default DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_BOTH
    help
        TX_ONLY on the bridge board and RX_ONLY on the hub board tests the real
        four-wire link between them. BOTH runs the whole thing on one board with
        three jumpers, which isolates the driver from the wiring.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_BOTH
        bool "Both (single board, self-loopback)"

    config DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_TX_ONLY
        bool "TX only (flash to the bridge board)"

    config DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_RX_ONLY
        bool "RX only (flash to the hub board)"
endchoice

config DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_RX_IS_MASTER
    bool "Invert clock ownership: RX generates BCLK/WS, TX follows"
    default n
    help
        Normally the transmitter is I2S master, so the receiver samples a clock
        from a foreign crystal. With this set the receiver generates the clocks
        and the transmitter follows, so the receiver's sampling is coherent with
        its own APB clock.

        MUST be set identically on both boards. No rewiring is needed -- BCLK and
        WS simply change which end drives them.

config DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_SLAVE_BCLK_INV
    bool "Slave drives data on the opposite BCLK edge"
    default n
    depends on DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_RX_IS_MASTER
    help
        With the receiver as master, the data path is a full round trip: master
        drives BCLK, slave sees it, slave drives DATA, master samples. That has
        to close inside half a BCLK period (355 us/1000 at 44.1 kHz 16-bit
        stereo), and across two boards it does not -- the symptom is every frame
        arriving but with wrong bit alignment (channel swaps).

        Inverting BCLK on the slave makes it drive data half a period earlier,
        which is the standard remedy. Set on BOTH boards.

config DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_DRIVE
    int "TX pin drive strength (0=~5mA, 1=~10mA, 2=~20mA default, 3=~40mA)"
    default 2
    depends on !DANCEFLOOR_LOOPBACK_RX_ONLY
    help
        Lower drive gives slower edges, which ring less on an unterminated link
        between two boards. Too low and the edges may not make it cleanly across
        a long wire, so it is worth A/B testing rather than assuming.

endmenu
